LS
ls  -> Lists all the files/directories in Current Directory 
ls -lrt  -> Lists all files/directories based on latest modification to a file/ directory in reverse order
ls -al  -> Lists all Hidden files along with Unhidden files

CD
cd  -> Change directory to a specific directory
cd..  -> change directory back to one level
cd ~  -> change directory to current user home directory
cd -  -> used to switch back to the previous working directory

pwd  -> shows present working directory

mkdir <directory_name>  -> creates new directory

RM
rm <filename>  -> remove the file
rm -r <directory_name>  -> removes the directory recursively
rm -rf <directory_name>  -> force remove the directory recursively

clear  -> used to clear the console 

CAT
cat <filename> -> display content of the given file
cat > a.txt -> creates a new file with filename a.txt and allows to enter content. Press ctrl+z to save the content to the file and exit

TAIL 
cat a.txt | tail -5  -> tail displays the last given number of lines of a file
ls -l | tail -3  -> displays the last given number of files in a directory

HEAD
cat a.txt | head -5  -> head displays the first given number of lines of a file
ls -l | head -3  -> displays the first given number of files in a directory

NANO
nano <filename>  -> Used to edit the file. If file is not present with given name, it will create one. Enter cmnd - nano <filename> and then enter content/edit file and then press ctrl+o to write to file and hit enter and then ctrl+x to exit

CP
cp <source_file> <dest_file>  -> copies content of files from source to destination. Example: cp a.txt b.txt. If destination file is not present it creates and then copy content from source to destination
cp -r <source_dir> <dest_dir> -> copies all files and directories recursively from source to destination. If destination directory is not present, it will create and copy the files.

MV
mv <source_path> <destination_path> -> moves file from source to destination. It is also used to rename filename within same path
